      "latency_ms": 2560.1,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 508,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a transparent, odorless, and tasteless chemical substance that is the main constituent of Earth's streams, lakes, and oceans, and the fluids of all known living organisms. \n\nTo truly understand water, it helps to look at it through a few different lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\nScientifically, water is a molecule made up of two types of atoms: **Hydrogen** and **Oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**. This means every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\n### 2. The Physical View (States of Matter)\nWater is famous for being the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states depending on the temperature:\n*   **Liquid:** The standard state of water we drink and swim in (at room temperature).\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Water freezes into ice at or below 0°C (32°F). Uniquely, solid water (ice) is less dense than liquid water, which is why ice floats.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Water turns into a gas (water vapor) at or above 100°C (212°F).\n\n### 3. The Ecological View (Earth's Lifeblood)\nWater covers about **71% of the Earth's surface**, mostly in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water). It is constantly moving through the **water cycle**—evaporating from the oceans, forming clouds, falling as rain or snow (precipitation), and flowing back to the oceans via rivers. \n\n### 4. The Biological View (Fuel for Life)\nWater is essential to all known forms of life. \n*   The human body is made up of about **60% water**.\n*   It acts as a universal solvent, meaning many substances dissolve in it, allowing chemical reactions to take place inside our cells.\n*   It regulates body temperature, lubricates joints, and flushes out waste.\n\n### Fun Fact: The \"Universal Solvent\"\nBecause of the way its molecules are shaped (with a slight positive charge on one side and a negative charge on the other), water is exceptionally good at dissolving other substances. This is why you rarely find \"pure\" water in nature"
